Lets extensions install from the Chrome Web Store and update from it, by undoing what
ungoogled-chromium does to the store's install and update path. Chromium's code comes back as it
was, except where noted:

- Domain substitution rewrote the store's hosts, so chromewebstore.google.com got neither
  webstorePrivate, which its "Add to Chrome" installs through, nor the management API, which its
  "Remove from Chrome" uses, and the XML namespace an update check's answer has to carry no longer
  matched Google's. The store's hosts, its update URL and the namespace are restored. Its API host
  (chromewebstore.googleapis.com), used only for store metadata and item snippets, stays
  substituted.
- inox-patchset's 0005-disable-default-extensions.patch empties WebstoreInstaller's download and
  install, and ungoogled-chromium's disable-webstore-urls.patch the downloader's update checks and
  the store's dedicated process. They're restored. The rest of disable-webstore-urls.patch stays:
  no pings, no OAuth token, no content verification, and no update check for an extension without
  an update URL.
- ungoogled-chromium's block-requests.patch stubs out update_client's update check, which Chromium
  updates store extensions with (UpdateService), so they update through ExtensionDownloader
  instead. That leaves the component updater off. Both changes are Helium's
  (patches/helium/core/reenable-update-checks.patch and proxy-extension-downloads.patch at
  imputnet/helium-chromium f29a25968e483f8bb85e47829ccd487bbe229199, GPL-3.0), without its proxy:
  the browser talks to Google's store directly (docs/decisions.md, decision 2).

